"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[8124],{88124:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"slug":"fed-podnyala-stavku-oktyabr-povyshenie","locale":"en","title":"Fed raises rates to 3.75–4%, with markets pricing more than 50% odds of another increase in October","description":"The Fed unanimously raised rates by a quarter-point last week to 3.75–4%. Markets put more than 50% odds on another October increase, and Minneapolis Fed President Neel Kashkari explained why: inflation has spread beyond the oil shock.","blocks":[{"id":"b001","kind":"paragraph","text":"Last week, the Federal Reserve unanimously raised its rate by 0.25 percentage points to a range of 3.75–4%. In projections published with the decision, all but two committee members expect at least one more quarter-point increase this year. Futures markets suggest the timing: the probability of an October increase is above 50%, and there is a two-thirds probability that the rate will end 2026 in the 4–4.25% range."},{"id":"b002","kind":"paragraph","text":"Major banks have already revised their forecasts. Goldman Sachs economists wrote on Wednesday that they now expect a second increase of 25 basis points, having previously viewed September\'s move as the only one. They said the meeting was more hawkish than expected, highlighting unanimity and Fed Chair Kevin Warsh\'s description of the increase as a removed dose of stimulus. Michael Goosay of Principal Asset Management argues that it may involve more than just one or two increases if the Fed seriously intends to destroy demand."},{"id":"b003","kind":"paragraph","text":"Neel Kashkari explained the regulator\'s position on Fox News on Sunday. Even excluding volatile energy and food, inflation remains too high, he said. Kashkari supported the increase and had been one of three officials voting for one at the previous meeting, when the majority left rates unchanged. His point is straightforward: price pressures have spread beyond the oil shock caused by the war in Iran."},{"id":"b004","kind":"paragraph","text":"Why does this distinction matter? If an apartment window is open in winter, the heating bill rises, but the radiator is not the problem. An oil-price spike works similarly: interest rates do not add tankers to a strait or directly lower gasoline prices. But if bread, haircuts and rent become more expensive with the window closed, the problem is inside the apartment itself. What remains after excluding energy and food is called core inflation, and Kashkari says it is too high. Rates can affect that: expensive credit reduces purchases and sellers lose their ability to raise prices."},{"id":"b005","kind":"paragraph","text":"The effects are already visible in debt markets. Last week, US government borrowing costs rose to their highest level since 2007, pulling up credit costs for households, companies and other governments. US public debt exceeds $40 trillion and oil costs more than $100 per barrel. Strategist Ed Yardeni reduced his year-end S&P 500 target because of this combination of expensive oil and expensive money."},{"id":"b006","kind":"paragraph","text":"For readers in Russia, the channel is indirect, through the dollar and oil, and these pull in different directions. High US rates make the dollar more attractive to global capital and strengthen it. A stronger dollar makes imports such as electronics, medicines and spare parts more expensive. Meanwhile, oil above $100 supports exporters\' revenue, the budget and therefore the ruble. Which channel will dominate is unknown. Russia\'s central bank sets its key rate according to domestic inflation, not Federal Reserve decisions."},{"id":"b007","kind":"paragraph","text":"What remains unknown is whether the Fed will act in October and whether the next inflation data will support Kashkari\'s remarks. Markets indicate a probability, not a fact. For an ordinary person, buying dollars on the rate news means buying alongside a market that has already priced that news in. Ignoring it can also be costly: if the Fed starts a series of increases and oil does not offset the effect, an expensive dollar becomes a year-long backdrop rather than an episode, filtering into import prices with a delay."}],"sources":[{"label":"https://finance.yahoo.com/economy/policy/article/this-could-be-something-more-substantial-wall-street-braces-for-more-fed-rate-hikes-123400710.html","url":"https://finance.yahoo.com/economy/policy/article/this-could-be-something-more-substantial-wall-street-braces-for-more-fed-rate-hikes-123400710.html"},{"label":"https://finance.yahoo.com/economy/policy/articles/feds-kashkari-says-inflation-goes-150738609.html","url":"https://finance.yahoo.com/economy/policy/articles/feds-kashkari-says-inflation-goes-150738609.html"},{"label":"https://www.reuters.com/business/feds-kashkari-says-inflation-goes-beyond-oil-prices-according-fox-news-interview-2026-09-20","url":"https://www.reuters.com/business/feds-kashkari-says-inflation-goes-beyond-oil-prices-according-fox-news-interview-2026-09-20"},{"label":"https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-09-20/fed-s-kashkari-says-inflation-reaching-all-corners-of-us-economy","url":"https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-09-20/fed-s-kashkari-says-inflation-reaching-all-corners-of-us-economy"},{"label":"https://www.theguardian.com/business/ng-interactive/2026/sep/20/stock-market-crash-government-bond-yields","url":"https://www.theguardian.com/business/ng-interactive/2026/sep/20/stock-market-crash-government-bond-yields"}],"sourceBodySha256":"4ccdae1c42dfa6caadccaf2dc660d7a7e9e7292252f88121ef27a0488828b560"}')}}]);